            0.815,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Algebraic Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "79"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.106,
            0.496,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.534,
            0.126,
            0.885,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 4.7 helps to visualise the product of $x \\quad + \\quad 3$ and $x \\; + \\; 4$ using algebra tiles. The expression $x + 3$ is represented using an x-tile and three unit tiles. Similarly $x \\quad + \\quad 4$ is represented using an x-tile and four unit tiles. The product of these two linear factors is shown in the inner rectangle comprising the $x \\mathrm { { - } t i l e } ,$ the x-tiles, and 12 unit tiles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.355,
            0.487,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 4.7: Factorisation o $f x ^ { 2 } + 7 x + 1 2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.383,
            0.887,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "Note that the 7x in $x^{2} + 7x + 12$ has been split as $3x \\pm 4x.$ This is represented by the fact that three x-tiles are placed on the right side of the x<sup>2</sup>-tile and four x-tiles are arranged below it. Also, the 12 unit tiles are arranged in a 3 by 4 array. Once the rectangular arrangement is formed, we observe that the dimensions of the rectangle are $x + 3$ and $x + 4$ units respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.517,
            0.399,
            0.54
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.553,
            0.855,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "Suppose 7x is split as $2x + 5x;$ can a similar rectangular arrangement be formed? Consider other possibilities and check."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.606,
            0.718,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 4.7 helps us to visualise two algebraic identities:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.635,
            0.883,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "(i) The linear expressions $x \\mp 3$ and $x + 4$ can be multiplied to obtain the identity $(x + 3)(x + 4) = x^{2} + 7x + 12.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.68,
            0.884,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Also the expression $x^{2} + 7x + 12$ can be factored into the linear factors $( x   +   3 )$ and $( x   +   4 )$ , giving the same identity $x^{2} + 7x + 12 =$ $(x + 3)(x + 4)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.756,
            0.396,
            0.78
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.794,
            0.768,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "Algebra tiles can be used to represent products and find factors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.816,
            0.765,
            0.837
          ],
          "content": "1. Figure out the product of $x + 2$ and $x + 3$ using algebra tiles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.84,
            0.854,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": "2. Lay out algebra tiles for $x^{2} + 11x + 30$ in such a way that you will see its factors."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
